      "question": "In the context of healthcare and heritage tourism, how do immersive technologies solve specific logistical or physical problems that traditional methods cannot?",
      "reference_answer": "In healthcare, immersive technologies address the shortage of cadavers by providing simulations for medical training on body organs. In heritage tourism, these technologies allow for 'virtual tours,' which solve the problem of physical decay and limited access; they enable users to explore historical sites virtually, preserving the physical integrity of the site while still allowing for high-volume exploration."
    },
    {
      "question": "Explain the distinction between 'non-immersive' and 'semi-immersive' systems. Why is a non-immersive system still considered a form of virtual reality?",
      "reference_answer": "Non-immersive systems are considered VR because they provide psychological immersion; even though the user's physical senses remain open to the real world, their attention is so fully captured by the digital environment (like a console game) that they become oblivious to their surroundings. Semi-immersive systems, by contrast, provide partial physical immersion by blocking specific senses—most commonly vision through a VR headset—while other senses remain free to perceive the real world."
    },
    {
      "question": "What are the primary trade-offs between fully immersive systems and semi-immersive systems regarding technology, cost, and commercial availability?",
      "reference_answer": "Fully immersive systems provide the highest degree of 'virtual realism' by using complex hardware (helmets, gloves, body suits, and motion trackers) to block all senses, but they are the most expensive and thus have lower commercial availability. Semi-immersive systems are more cost-effective and are currently the most common commercial applications because they offer a balance of immersion (blocking vision) without the extreme cost of full-body sensory blockade."
    }
  ]
}

