{
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "How does the concept of attaching a C# script as a component differ from using Unity's built-in editor support in terms of developer experience?",
      "reference_answer": "Unity's built-in support provides a standard, pre-configured experience suitable for common tasks, but it is limited to the functionalities provided by the engine. By attaching a script as a component, a developer creates a customized experience where they can trigger specific game events, modify properties over time, and respond to user inputs in a way that is uniquely tailored to their specific needs rather than relying on the system's default behavior."
    },
    {
      "question": "Explain the distinction between a standard function and a coroutine when performing a time-sensitive task like fading an object's opacity, and why this distinction is critical for VR/AR applications.",
      "reference_answer": "A standard function executes its logic in a single frame, which might happen so quickly that the transition (like a fade) is not perceivable by the user. A coroutine, however, allows a developer to spread a task across several frames by pausing execution and returning control to Unity. In immersive environments like VR/AR, this is critical for creating smooth, perceivable visual transitions and animations that occur over time."
    },
    {
      "question": "How does the integration of an SDK (like Vuforia) illustrate the layered architecture of a virtual or augmented reality system?",
      "reference_answer": "The integration of an SDK illustrates the layered architecture by placing a specialized software layer between the Unity engine and the underlying hardware/libraries. While Unity provides the core environment (middleware), an SDK provides specific, advanced functionalities (like computer vision or device-specific sensors) that are not part of the built-in support. This allows developers to bridge the gap between high-level application logic and low-level hardware capabilities without having to write the foundation-level code from scratch."
    }
  ]
}

